        "## 1. Importing PyTorch"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "nM7LC3m1pRfv"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import torch\n",
        "print(torch.__version__)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Tensors are the fundamental building block of machine learning.\n",
        "\n",
        "Their job is to represent data in a numerical way.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, you could represent an image as a tensor with shape `[3, 224, 224]` which would mean `[colour_channels, height, width]`, as in the image has `3` colour channels (red, green, blue), a height of `224` pixels and a width of `224` pixels.\n",
        "\n",
        "![example of going from an input image to a tensor representation of the image, image gets broken down into 3 colour channels as well as numbers to represent the height and width](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mrdbourke/pytorch-deep-learning/main/images/00-tensor-shape-example-of-image.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "In tensor-speak (the language used to describe tensors), the tensor would have three dimensions, one for `colour_channels`, `height` and `width`.\n",
        "\n",
        "But we're getting ahead of ourselves.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's learn more about tensors by coding them."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0_TbcCc-r9OU"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AJaXeKwOpRfw"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 2. What is a tensor?\n",
        "\n",
        "A **tensor** is a container of numbers. It is the basic building block of deep learning: images, text, audio and tables are all turned into tensors before a model can use them.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, a colour image can be stored as a tensor of shape `[3, 224, 224]`, meaning `[colour_channels, height, width]`.\n",
        "\n",
        "Tensors come in different numbers of dimensions:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Name | What is it? | Number of dimensions (`ndim`) |\n",
        "| ----- | ----- | ----- |\n",
        "| **scalar** | a single number | 0 |\n",
        "| **vector** | a list of numbers | 1 |\n",
        "| **matrix** | a table of numbers (rows and columns) | 2 |\n",
        "| **tensor** | numbers arranged in any number of dimensions | 0, 1, 2, 3, ... |\n",
        "\n",
        "![scalar vector matrix tensor and what they look like](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mrdbourke/pytorch-deep-learning/main/images/00-scalar-vector-matrix-tensor.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "Two attributes describe every tensor:\n",
        "* `ndim` - how many dimensions it has\n",
        "* `shape` - how many elements there are along each dimension"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "_T9TpdQ5pRfw"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Scalar: a single number, 0 dimensions\n",
        "scalar = torch.tensor(7)\n",
        "print(scalar)\n",
        "print(\"ndim: \", scalar.ndim)\n",
        "print(\"shape:\", scalar.shape)\n",
        "print(\"as a Python number:\", scalar.item())   # .item() only works on one-element tensors"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "G3nl3YQIpRfw"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Vector: 1 dimension\n",
        "vector = torch.tensor([7, 7])\n",
        "print(vector)\n",
        "print(\"ndim: \", vector.ndim)\n",
        "print(\"shape:\", vector.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yT-7wv1HpRfx"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Matrix: 2 dimensions (rows, columns)\n",
        "MATRIX = torch.tensor([[7, 8],\n",
        "                       [9, 10]])\n",
        "print(MATRIX)\n",
        "print(\"ndim: \", MATRIX.ndim)\n",
        "print(\"shape:\", MATRIX.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "eLtNVQ2zpRfx"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Tensor: 3 dimensions here\n",
        "TENSOR = torch.tensor([[[1, 2, 3],\n",
        "                        [3, 6, 9],\n",
        "                        [2, 4, 5]]])\n",
        "print(TENSOR)\n",
        "print(\"ndim: \", TENSOR.ndim)\n",
        "print(\"shape:\", TENSOR.shape)   # 1 block of 3 rows and 3 columns"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "TUJG0JbnpRfx"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Tip:** the number of dimensions equals the number of opening square brackets `[` at the start of the tensor. `[[[` means 3 dimensions.\n",
        "\n",
        "The shape is read from the outside in: `torch.Size([1, 3, 3])` means 1 block, each with 3 rows, each with 3 values."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "DmV5fogcpRfx"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 3. Creating tensors\n",
        "\n",
        "You will rarely type tensors by hand. A neural network starts with tensors full of **random numbers** and then adjusts them as it learns:\n",
        "\n",
        "`start with random numbers -> look at data -> update the numbers -> look at data -> update the numbers ...`\n",
        "\n",
        "The most useful functions for creating tensors are:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Function | What it creates |\n",
        "| ----- | ----- |\n",
        "| `torch.rand(size)` | random numbers between 0 and 1 |\n",
        "| `torch.zeros(size)` | all zeros |\n",
        "| `torch.ones(size)` | all ones |\n",
        "| `torch.arange(start, end, step)` | a range of numbers (`end` is not included) |\n",
        "| `torch.zeros_like(x)`, `torch.ones_like(x)` | zeros or ones with the same shape as `x` |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ldWvvw6cpRfx"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Random tensor with 3 rows and 4 columns\n",
        "random_tensor = torch.rand(size=(3, 4))\n",
        "print(random_tensor)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Random tensor in the shape of an image: [colour_channels, height, width]\n",
        "random_image = torch.rand(size=(3, 224, 224))\n",
        "print(random_image.shape, random_image.ndim)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "jXMRMKZWpRfy"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Zeros and ones\n",
        "zeros = torch.zeros(size=(3, 4))\n",
        "ones = torch.ones(size=(3, 4))\n",
        "print(zeros)\n",
        "print(ones)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "zM9pxA6GpRfy"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# A range of numbers: start=0, end=10 (not included), step=1\n",
        "zero_to_nine = torch.arange(start=0, end=10, step=1)\n",
        "print(zero_to_nine)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Zeros with the same shape as another tensor\n",
        "print(torch.zeros_like(zero_to_nine))"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "tsCrkcr2pRfy"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 4. Tensor datatypes\n",
        "\n",
        "Every tensor has a datatype (`dtype`). The ones you will meet most often:\n",
        "\n",
        "| dtype | Meaning | When you see it |\n",
        "| ----- | ----- | ----- |\n",
        "| `torch.float32` | decimal numbers, 32 bits | the default for decimal values, used by most models |\n",
        "| `torch.int64` | whole numbers, 64 bits | the default for whole numbers, used for labels and indexes |\n",
        "| `torch.float16` | decimal numbers, 16 bits | smaller and faster, but less precise |\n",
        "\n",
        "You choose the datatype with the `dtype` parameter, and you change it later with `.type()`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "f9bcgfTupRfy"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Decimal values give float32 by default\n",
        "float_32_tensor = torch.tensor([3.0, 6.0, 9.0])\n",
        "print(float_32_tensor.dtype)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Whole numbers give int64 by default\n",
        "int_tensor = torch.tensor([3, 6, 9])\n",
        "print(int_tensor.dtype)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Choose the datatype when creating the tensor\n",
        "float_16_tensor = torch.tensor([3.0, 6.0, 9.0], dtype=torch.float16)\n",
        "print(float_16_tensor.dtype)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Change the datatype of an existing tensor\n",
        "converted = int_tensor.type(torch.float32)\n",
        "print(converted, converted.dtype)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "JlaWy1WIpRfy"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 5. Getting information from a tensor\n",
        "\n",
        "Most PyTorch errors come from one of three things. When something breaks, check them in this order:\n",
        "\n",
        "* `shape` - **what shape** is the tensor?\n",
        "* `dtype` - **what datatype** is it?\n",
        "* `device` - **where** is it stored (CPU or GPU)?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "b1ergYBCpRfy"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "some_tensor = torch.rand(3, 4)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(some_tensor)\n",
        "print(f\"Shape of tensor: {some_tensor.shape}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Datatype of tensor: {some_tensor.dtype}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Device tensor is stored on: {some_tensor.device}\")   # CPU by default"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hFsm8BhMpRfy"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 6. Tensor operations\n",
        "\n",
        "A neural network is built from a small set of operations on tensors: addition, subtraction, multiplication, division and **matrix multiplication**.\n",
        "\n",
        "### 6.1 Basic operations\n",
        "\n",
        "These work element by element."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "zLPb3DGBpRfy"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "tensor = torch.tensor([1, 2, 3])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(tensor + 10)   # add 10 to every element\n",
        "print(tensor - 10)   # subtract 10 from every element\n",
        "print(tensor * 10)   # multiply every element by 10\n",
        "print(tensor / 10)   # divide every element by 10\n",
        "\n",
        "# The original tensor does not change unless you reassign it\n",
        "print(tensor)\n",
        "tensor = tensor + 10\n",
        "print(tensor)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "4MjO3luIpRfy"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 6.2 Element-wise multiplication vs matrix multiplication\n",
        "\n",
        "For a tensor with values `[1, 2, 3]`:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Operation | Calculation | Code |\n",
        "| ----- | ----- | ----- |\n",
        "| **Element-wise multiplication** | `[1*1, 2*2, 3*3]` = `[1, 4, 9]` | `tensor * tensor` |\n",
        "| **Matrix multiplication** | `1*1 + 2*2 + 3*3` = `14` | `torch.matmul(tensor, tensor)` or `tensor @ tensor` |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "YYWs1ulZpRfy"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "tensor = torch.tensor([1, 2, 3])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Element-wise:\", tensor * tensor)\n",
        "print(\"Matrix multiplication:\", torch.matmul(tensor, tensor))\n",
        "print(\"Matrix multiplication with @:\", tensor @ tensor)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "5ieRAouepRfy"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 6.3 The two rules of matrix multiplication\n",
        "\n",
        "1. The **inner dimensions** must match:\n",
        "  * `(3, 2) @ (3, 2)` will not work\n",
        "  * `(2, 3) @ (3, 2)` will work\n",
        "  * `(3, 2) @ (2, 3)` will work\n",
        "2. The result has the shape of the **outer dimensions**:\n",
        "  * `(2, 3) @ (3, 2)` -> `(2, 2)`\n",
        "  * `(3, 2) @ (2, 3)` -> `(3, 3)`\n",
        "\n",
        "Breaking rule 1 gives a **shape error**, one of the most common errors in deep learning."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "MOvdQ9UupRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "tensor_A = torch.tensor([[1., 2.],\n",
        "                         [3., 4.],\n",
        "                         [5., 6.]])     # shape (3, 2)\n",
        "\n",
        "tensor_B = torch.tensor([[7., 10.],\n",
        "                         [8., 11.],\n",
        "                         [9., 12.]])    # shape (3, 2)\n",
        "\n",
        "# (3, 2) @ (3, 2): the inner dimensions 2 and 3 do not match, so this fails\n",
        "try:\n",
        "    torch.matmul(tensor_A, tensor_B)\n",
        "except RuntimeError as error:\n",
        "    print(\"Error:\", error)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HjJkhKCspRfz"
      },
      "source": [
        "To fix it, **transpose** one of the tensors with `.T`. Transposing swaps rows and columns, so a `(3, 2)` tensor becomes `(2, 3)`."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "meGJ-gZUpRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "print(\"tensor_B shape:  \", tensor_B.shape)\n",
        "print(\"tensor_B.T shape:\", tensor_B.T.shape)\n",
        "\n",
        "# (3, 2) @ (2, 3): the inner dimensions match, the result is (3, 3)\n",
        "output = torch.matmul(tensor_A, tensor_B.T)\n",
        "print(output)\n",
        "print(\"Output shape:\", output.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "fq_0YomSpRfz"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 7. Aggregation (min, max, mean, sum)\n",
        "\n",
        "Aggregation turns many values into fewer values.\n",
        "\n",
        "`argmin()` and `argmax()` return the **position (index)** of the smallest and largest value, not the value itself."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "998_1CaDpRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "x = torch.arange(0, 100, 10)\n",
        "print(x)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Minimum: {x.min()}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Maximum: {x.max()}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Sum: {x.sum()}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "# mean() needs a float tensor, so convert the datatype first\n",
        "print(f\"Mean: {x.type(torch.float32).mean()}\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"Index of the minimum value: {x.argmin()}\")\n",
        "print(f\"Index of the maximum value: {x.argmax()}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cOgSWGFdpRfz"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 8. Changing the shape of a tensor\n",
        "\n",
        "Models need tensors in the right shape. These methods change the shape without changing the values:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Method | What it does |\n",
        "| ----- | ----- |\n",
        "| `x.reshape(shape)` | gives `x` a new shape (the total number of elements must stay the same) |\n",
        "| `torch.stack([a, b], dim)` | joins tensors along a **new** dimension |\n",
        "| `x.squeeze()` | removes all dimensions of size 1 |\n",
        "| `x.unsqueeze(dim)` | adds a dimension of size 1 at position `dim` |\n",
        "| `x.permute(dims)` | rearranges the order of the dimensions |"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "SxnwUzVApRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "x = torch.arange(1., 9.)\n",
        "print(x, x.shape)\n",
        "\n",
        "# reshape: 8 elements can become 2 rows of 4\n",
        "x_reshaped = x.reshape(2, 4)\n",
        "print(x_reshaped, x_reshaped.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Sri1mvhwpRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# stack: join tensors along a new dimension\n",
        "a = torch.tensor([1, 2, 3])\n",
        "b = torch.tensor([4, 5, 6])\n",
        "\n",
        "print(torch.stack([a, b], dim=0))   # a and b become rows,    shape (2, 3)\n",
        "print(torch.stack([a, b], dim=1))   # a and b become columns, shape (3, 2)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "qGVnN9GrpRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# squeeze: remove dimensions of size 1\n",
        "y = torch.rand(1, 5)\n",
        "y_squeezed = y.squeeze()\n",
        "print(\"Before squeeze:\", y.shape)\n",
        "print(\"After squeeze: \", y_squeezed.shape)\n",
        "\n",
        "# unsqueeze: add a dimension of size 1\n",
        "y_unsqueezed = y_squeezed.unsqueeze(dim=0)\n",
        "print(\"After unsqueeze(dim=0):\", y_unsqueezed.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "K5c82ooYpRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# permute: rearrange the dimensions\n",
        "# Example: an image stored as [height, width, colour_channels]\n",
        "image = torch.rand(size=(224, 224, 3))\n",
        "\n",
        "# PyTorch models expect [colour_channels, height, width]\n",
        "image_permuted = image.permute(2, 0, 1)   # old dimension 2 goes first, then 0, then 1\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Before permute:\", image.shape)\n",
        "print(\"After permute: \", image_permuted.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "xbhxpZnzpRfz"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 9. Indexing (selecting data from tensors)\n",
        "\n",
        "Indexing works like Python lists and NumPy arrays. You index from the outer dimension to the inner dimension. Use `:` to mean \"everything in this dimension\"."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`torch.Size([1, 3, 3])`.\n",
        "\n",
        "The dimensions go outer to inner.\n",
        "\n",
        "That means there's 1 dimension of 3 by 3.\n",
        "\n",
        "![example of different tensor dimensions](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mrdbourke/pytorch-deep-learning/main/images/00-pytorch-different-tensor-dimensions.png)\n",
        "\n",
        "> **Note:** You might've noticed me using lowercase letters for `scalar` and `vector` and uppercase letters for `MATRIX` and `TENSOR`. This was on purpose. In practice, you'll often see scalars and vectors denoted as lowercase letters such as `y` or `a`. And matrices and tensors denoted as uppercase letters such as `X` or `W`.\n",
        ">\n",
        "> You also might notice the names martrix and tensor used interchangably. This is common. Since in PyTorch you're often dealing with `torch.Tensor`s (hence the tensor name), however, the shape and dimensions of what's inside will dictate what it actually is.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "WXUuFSrYqnj5"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3TemuKgFpRfz"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "x = torch.arange(1, 10).reshape(1, 3, 3)\n",
        "print(x, x.shape)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
