      "source": [
        "## 10. PyTorch and NumPy\n",
        "\n",
        "Data often arrives as a NumPy array, and plotting libraries expect NumPy arrays, so you need to convert in both directions:\n",
        "\n",
        "* `torch.from_numpy(array)` - NumPy array -> PyTorch tensor\n",
        "* `tensor.numpy()` - PyTorch tensor -> NumPy array\n",
        "\n",
        "Note: NumPy uses `float64` by default while PyTorch uses `float32`. The conversion keeps the original datatype."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "Yuk0hK42pRf0"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# NumPy array -> tensor\n",
        "array = np.arange(1.0, 8.0)\n",
        "tensor = torch.from_numpy(array)\n",
        "print(array, array.dtype)\n",
        "print(tensor)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Convert to float32 if you need the PyTorch default\n",
        "print(tensor.type(torch.float32).dtype)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Tensor -> NumPy array\n",
        "tensor = torch.ones(7)\n",
        "numpy_array = tensor.numpy()\n",
        "print(numpy_array, numpy_array.dtype)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "pWlKW--ppRf0"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 11. Reproducibility (random seed)\n",
        "\n",
        "`torch.rand()` gives different numbers every time. That makes experiments hard to repeat.\n",
        "\n",
        "`torch.manual_seed(seed)` fixes the starting point of the random number generator, so the same seed always gives the same \"random\" numbers.\n",
        "\n",
        "Important: call `torch.manual_seed()` again before **each** random call that you want to repeat."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2w7Uz_LTpRf1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Without a seed: two different tensors\n",
        "random_tensor_A = torch.rand(3, 4)\n",
        "random_tensor_B = torch.rand(3, 4)\n",
        "print(random_tensor_A == random_tensor_B)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "yG8K-dNXpRf1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# With a seed: two identical tensors\n",
        "RANDOM_SEED = 42\n",
        "\n",
        "torch.manual_seed(RANDOM_SEED)\n",
        "random_tensor_C = torch.rand(3, 4)\n",
        "\n",
        "torch.manual_seed(RANDOM_SEED)    # set the seed again before the second call\n",
        "random_tensor_D = torch.rand(3, 4)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(random_tensor_C)\n",
        "print(random_tensor_D)\n",
        "print(random_tensor_C == random_tensor_D)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "9enDwwGcpRf1"
      },
      "source": [
        "## 12. Running tensors on a GPU\n",
        "\n",
        "A GPU does the maths of neural networks (mainly matrix multiplication) much faster than a CPU.\n",
        "\n",
        "The standard pattern is **device-agnostic code**: use the GPU if there is one, otherwise use the CPU. The same code then runs on any computer."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "FzfpWO5npRf1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "# Is a GPU available?\n",
        "print(torch.cuda.is_available())\n",
        "\n",
        "# Device-agnostic setup\n",
        "device = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\"\n",
        "print(device)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "cQy07rF0pRf1"
      },
      "source": [
        "Move a tensor to a device with `.to(device)`. This returns a copy, so reassign it if you want to keep the result.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two tensors must be on the **same device** before you can combine them."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "QXc7f5qbpRf1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "tensor = torch.tensor([1, 2, 3])\n",
        "print(tensor, tensor.device)\n",
        "\n",
        "tensor_on_device = tensor.to(device)\n",
        "print(tensor_on_device, tensor_on_device.device)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "-XnVkZumpRf1"
      },
      "source": [
        "NumPy only works on the CPU. Calling `.numpy()` on a GPU tensor gives an error, so bring the tensor back with `.cpu()` first."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {
        "id": "uMENTGmHpRf1"
      },
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "tensor_back_on_cpu = tensor_on_device.cpu().numpy()\n",
        "print(tensor_back_on_cpu)"
      ]
    },
